  <title>Identify Positive and Negative Trends: Mini-Lesson Review</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m00677</md:content-id>
    <md:title>Identify Positive and Negative Trends: Mini-Lesson Review</md:title>
    <md:uuid>e5842f4d-db92-480c-a60b-99e4f5308cb2</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>Question #2: Identify Positive and Negative Trends</title>
      <para id="para-00001">The scatter plot shows data on weight in pounds (x) and fuel efficiency in miles per
        gallon (y) for 13 cars.</para>

        <media alt="points (2500, 28), (3259,23), (3500,25), (3559,22), (3550,18), (3700,20), (4000,20), (4000,18), (4400,16), (4500,16), (4750, 14), (5250, 12), (5700,15)">
          <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_03_02_05_01.jpg"/>
        </media>
      <para id="para-00002">Which statement best describes the data?</para>
    </section>

    <section id="sect-00002">
      <title>Identify Positive and Negative Trends </title>
      <para id="para-00003">Scatter plots may have a strong linear trend or a weak linear trend. A strong positive
        trend means as one variable increases, the other variable increases, A strong negative trend
        means as one variable increases, the other variable decreases. A weak linear pattern means
        the points are more scattered.</para>
    
        <media alt="">
          <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_03_02_06_01.jpg"/>
        </media>
 </section>
        <note class="try" id="note-00001">
      <title>Try It: Identify Positive and Negative Trends</title>
        <media alt="">
          <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_03_02_06_02.png"/>
        </media>
      
      <!--Text
      Entry Interaction Start -->
      <exercise id="exer-00001">
        <problem id="prob-00001">
          <para id="para-00004">Does the scatter plot show a positive or negative trend? Is the relationship weak or
            strong?</para>
        </problem>
        <solution id="sol-00001">
          <para id="para-00005">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to
            compare your work.</para>
          <para id="para-00006">Here is how to describe the relationship:</para>
          <para id="para-00007">As the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-values increase, the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-values decrease. The trend is negative. The
            relationship is linear, but the points are scattered, so the relationship is weak.</para>
        </solution>
      </exercise>
    </note>
      <!--Interaction
      End -->
      <!--inserted
      mini review question Begin -->
      <section class="check-understanding" id="sect-00003"><title>Check Your Understanding</title>
        <exercise id="_33592150-3516-4f57-a9e6-6351e6ee7c9a" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00002">
            <para id="para-00008">The scatter plot shows the relationship between crocodiles body mass in pounds,
              <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, and bite force in pounds, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>. Which statement best describes the
              relationship between body mass and bite force?</para>
              <media alt="">
                <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_03_02_06_03.jpg"/>
              </media>
                
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00002">
            <para id="para-00009">Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: There does appear to be a positive
              relationship, but that is because both quantities are increasing. The answer is there
              appears to be a positive relationship between bite force and body mass. The greater
              the body mass, the greater the bite force tends to be.</para>
              <para id="para-00010">Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: When there is a negative relationship, one quantity increases while another decreases. The answer is there appears to be a positive relationship between bite force and body mass. The greater the body mass, the greater the bite force tends to be.</para>
              <para id="para-00011">Incorrect. Let’s try again a different way: looking at the scatter plot, as the body mass increases, so does the bite force. The answer is there appears to be a positive relationship between bite force and body mass. The greater the body mass, the greater the bite force tends to be.</para>
              <para id="para-00012">There appears to be a positive relationship between bite force and body mass. The greater the body mass, the greater the bite force tends to be.</para>
              <para id="para-00013">That’s correct! Check yourself: As the body mass of the crocodiles increases, so does the bite force. Since both quantities are increasing, there appears to be a positive relationship.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </section>

      <!--inserted
      mini review question END -->
